module gunbc.rostered_row_join

import std.types { String, List }

// THE DECLARED-VERSUS-ROSTERED IDENTITY JOIN: what it enrolls, what it can decide, and what it
// deliberately does not claim.
//
// THE SUBJECT. A carrier whose rows live one per module and whose roster re-enumerates them by hand
// has two authorings of one membership fact, so a row that lands DECLARED and UNROSTERED is absent
// from every projection derived from that roster and nothing refuses it.
// `gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster_re_enumerates_its_own_rows_stall` is that class, and this module is
// its declared next-rung trigger executed: the declared population read through
// `v2.std.decl_index` `data_decl_type_facts`, joined against the roster AT DECLARATION IDENTITY,
// refusing with the located identity of each declared row the roster omits.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT, said first because it is the reading a reader arrives with. It does NOT remove
// the double authoring. After this join every author still declares membership twice -- once by
// writing the row file, once by adding the import and the list entry. What changes is that the
// OMISSION IS LOUD instead of silent. The stall's ceiling is `StructurallyImpossible` and is reached
// by VALUE BINDING -- folding the roster over the declared population so there is no list to omit
// from -- which needs a capability that does not exist (see that row's own annotation). This is the
// mechanically-preventable rung: the invalid state stays perfectly writable and safety depends on
// this check executing and staying enrolled.
//
// WHY THE ROW TYPES ARE ENUMERATED HERE AND THE CHECK IS NOT. A `.dag` consumer cannot perform this
// join at all, and the reason is the same missing capability: `data_decl_type_facts` reports that a
// declaration EXISTS with a declared type, and the roster's list literal is a list of VALUES that do
// not carry the names they were bound from -- `DeclFact`'s initializer projection reports the same
// kind for a literal and for a reference to another declaration. So no Accepted program can ask
// "which declarations does this list name". The names are readable only from the parse, which is
// where the join therefore executes: `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join`, inside the required-CI
// PARSE phase, riding the index that phase has already built. That is the same shape and the same
// reason as `claim_executor` `phase_roster_findings`, which joins the host phase enum against
// `gunbc.required_ci_phase_roster` on every required run.
//
// `type_name` IS A STRING AND THAT IS DISCOVERY EVIDENCE, NOT TYPED MEMBERSHIP. `DataDeclTypeFact`
// carries the AUTHORED HEAD NAME of the declared type annotation, so this join compares spellings.
// Two distinct types spelled alike in different modules are indistinguishable to it, and a row whose
// annotation is written through an alias is invisible to it. Calling this a typed membership check
// would be the section 4b(1) inflation this corpus names; it is a spelling join over a population
// discovered by parse, and its refusals are exact only because the enrolled spellings are, today,
// declared in exactly one module each.
//
// ELIGIBILITY IS DERIVED FROM THE ROSTER'S OWN HOME, NOT DECLARED PER ROW. A membership boolean on
// the row -- `include_in_roster: true` -- would be one more place to omit the row, which is the
// defect itself wearing a fix's clothes. So the subject of each enrolled row type is: every
// declaration of that type whose module has THE SAME CARRIER CLASS as the module declaring the
// roster, where carrier class is `v1_compiler.declaration_index` `module_is_fixture_carrier` -- the
// corpus's single authority for "this module is a witness or fixture". A production roster therefore
// ranges over production declarations and cannot be reddened by a witness's planted specimen, and a
// FIXTURE roster ranges over fixture declarations, which is what makes the control below executable
// at all.
//
// TWO CONTROLS ARE ENROLLED IN THE SAME LIST AS THE PRODUCTION ROWS, and per section 4b(4) neither
// retires when the wall lands. `test.fixture.rostered_row_join.omitted_control` declares two rows
// and rosters one; the join must find exactly the omitted one. The collision control is the SECOND
// and it discriminates something the first cannot: two declarations sharing a BARE NAME in
// different modules, only one rostered. A drop-one-row control passes whether membership is keyed
// on a spelling or on a declaration identity -- the keys agree whenever names are distinct, which
// they were across the whole corpus when this join landed -- so only a collision is evidence that
// the module component is load-bearing. It is enrolled because the bare-name key was the shipped
// behaviour and was found in review of gunbc#10496. A fixture-homed enrolled type whose join
// finds NOTHING stops the line, because that is the state in which the check has gone green by
// construction -- the only state a merge-blocking check cannot be allowed to reach quietly. The
// accepting positive control is the production side of the same run: three rosters that must join
// completely, with their declared and rostered populations printed beside the verdict.
type RosteredRowType
  = GuaranteeStallRows
  | RecurringFailureModeRows
  | RungDropRows
  | OmittedRowControl
  | CollisionRowControl

// One enrolled row type: the declared-type spelling `data_decl_type_facts` reports, and the
// declaration that rosters it. The roster is named by MODULE AND DECLARATION rather than by path,
// per the section 3 standing rule; an absent module or an absent declaration REFUSES, so a
// misspelling here stops the line rather than narrowing the subject to nothing.
type RosteredRowJoin {
  row_type: RosteredRowType
  declared_type_name: String
  roster_module: String
  roster_declaration: String
}

data rostered_row_joins: List<RosteredRowJoin> = [
  RosteredRowJoin {
    row_type: GuaranteeStallRows,
    declared_type_name: "GuaranteeStall",
    roster_module: "gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster",
    roster_declaration: "all_guarantee_stalls",
  },
  RosteredRowJoin {
    row_type: RecurringFailureModeRows,
    declared_type_name: "RecurringFailureMode",
    roster_module: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster",
    roster_declaration: "recurring_failure_mode_roster",
  },
  RosteredRowJoin {
    row_type: RungDropRows,
    declared_type_name: "RungDrop",
    roster_module: "gunbc.rung_drop.roster",
    roster_declaration: "rung_drop_roster",
  },
  RosteredRowJoin {
    row_type: CollisionRowControl,
    declared_type_name: "CollisionControlRow",
    roster_module: "test.fixture.rostered_row_join.collision_control_roster",
    roster_declaration: "collision_control_roster",
  },
  RosteredRowJoin {
    row_type: OmittedRowControl,
    declared_type_name: "OmittedControlRow",
    roster_module: "test.fixture.rostered_row_join.omitted_control",
    roster_declaration: "omitted_control_roster",
  },
]
